course: ukrainian-street
lesson: 18
title: I know! The head — and happy, angry, bored, scared
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: In class a hand shoots up: "ya znAyu!" — I know! — and the worst words in school are "tebe do dyrEktora!", you're sent to the head.
explain: Feelings in Ukrainian often come with "meni", "to me": "meni nUdno" is literally "to me it's boring", so it means I'm bored; "meni strAshno" means I'm scared; "meni sumno" means I'm sad.
explain: Other feelings use "ya", I: "ya radyi" (I'm glad), "ya zlyi" (I'm angry), "ya vtomYvsya" (I'm tired) — and a girl changes the ending: "ya rada", "ya vtomylasya".
explain: When Sashko looks upset, the kindest thing you can ask is "shcho stAlosya?", what happened? When he's angry, say "ne zlYsya", and when he's scared, "ne bIisya".
fact: "ya znAyu!" means "I know!" and is what kids call out with a hand up in class.
fact: "dyrEktor" means the head teacher; "tebe do dyrEktora!" means you are sent to the head, and "vyklykaly batkiv", they called the parents in, means real trouble.
fact: "ya radyi" means "I'm glad, I'm happy" when a boy says it; a girl says "ya rada", and the word has nothing to do with "radio".
fact: "shchaslYvyi" means happy or lucky, as in "ya taky shchaslYvyi!", I'm so happy; it has two shch-sounds, so say it slowly.
fact: "ya zlyi" means "I'm angry" when a boy says it, and the natural answer is "chomu?", why?
fact: "zlyi" also means wicked or mean, so "zlyi pes" is a vicious dog, not an angry-for-a-minute dog.
fact: "ne zlYsya" means "don't be angry" and is used to calm a friend down.
fact: "meni nUdno" means "I'm bored", literally "to me it's boring", and a friend may answer "daVAI hrAty!", let's play!
fact: "meni strAshno" means "I'm scared", and the answer is "ne bIisya", don't be afraid.
fact: "ya vtomYvsya" means "I'm tired" when a boy says it; a girl says "ya vtomylasya".
fact: "meni sumno" means "I'm sad", and a good friend answers "shcho stAlosya?".
fact: "shcho stAlosya?" means "what happened? what's wrong?" — a kind question to ask a friend who looks upset.
exercise: The teacher asks a question and Sashko's hand shoots up. What does he shout? | ya znAyu! | ne znAyu | ya ne rozuMIyu | meni nUdno
exercise: Who is the "dyrEktor" at school? | The head teacher | The class teacher | The caretaker | The school bus driver
exercise: A teacher says "tebe do dyrEktora!". What does it mean? | You're sent to the head | You've won a prize | Go back to your seat | It's time for break
exercise: Sashko's birthday party is tomorrow and he says "ya radyi". How does he feel? | Glad, happy | Angry, cross | Tired, sleepy | Scared, nervous
exercise: How would Sashko's sister say "I'm glad"? | ya rada | ya radyi | ya radio | ya zlyi
exercise: What does "shchaslYvyi" mean? | Happy or lucky | Sad and lonely | Scared of the dark | Hungry and tired
exercise: Sashko stomps in and says "ya zlyi". What is the natural answer? | chomu? | DYAkuyu! | krutO! | molodEts!
exercise: What does "zlyi pes" on a gate warn you about? | A vicious dog | A sad, lonely dog | A sleepy old dog | A lost dog
exercise: Your friend is fuming after losing a game. What do you say to calm him? | ne zlYsya | ne bIisya | ne plAch | meni nUdno
exercise: It's raining, nothing to do, and Sashko sighs "meni nUdno". How does he feel? | Bored | Scared | Sad, gloomy | Hungry
exercise: Sashko says "meni nUdno". What is a good friendly answer? | daVAI hrAty! | ne bIisya, ne plAch | proBAch | DOdomu!
exercise: A big dog barks at Sashko's little brother, who whispers "meni strAshno". How does he feel? | Scared | Bored stiff | Angry, cross | Glad, happy
exercise: Your friend is scared of a thunderstorm. What do you tell him? | ne bIisya | ne zlYsya | ya znAyu! | krutO!
exercise: After football Sashko flops on the grass: "ya vtomYvsya". What does he mean? | I'm tired | I've won the game | I'm hurt, my knee | I'm thirsty
exercise: Sashko's dog is ill and he says "meni sumno". What do you ask? | shcho stAlosya? | yak teBE zvut? | SKILky tobI rokIv? | de tualEt?
exercise: What does "shcho stAlosya?" mean? | What happened? What's wrong? | What time is it? | What is this called? | What's for dinner?
test: my bro says "ya radyi" means "I'm on the radio" lol. right? | No, it means I'm glad | Yes, it's about the radio | Yes, it means I'm loud | No, it means I'm angry
test: A girl in a story says "ya vtomYvsya". What should she say? | ya vtomylasya | ya vtomYvsya is right for girls too | ya rada | meni vtomYvsya
test: Sashko is crying behind the shed. Which question is kindest? | shcho stAlosya? | ty shcho, durnyi? | SKILky tobI rokIv? | de ty zhyvEsh?
test: Teacher says "vyklykaly batkiv" about a kid. How serious is that? | Real trouble: the parents were called in | Not at all, he won a prize | A small joke, nobody minds | He just forgot his pen
test: Why is "meni nUdno" a strange shape to an English ear? | It literally says "to me it's boring" | It literally says "I am a boredom" | It is a question, not a sentence | It is only used by teachers
test: A sign on a farm says "zlyi pes". Your mate says "aww, the dog's just a bit grumpy, go stroke it". Should you? | No, it means a vicious dog: keep away | Yes, zlyi only means sad | Yes, it means a friendly dog | No, it means the dog is asleep
test: shut up, i'm not scared, says Sashko's cousin, then whispers "meni strAshno". What does he really feel? | Scared | Bored stiff | Happy and calm | Angry, furious
test: Sashko is not scared at all, just furious that you won. Which is the right thing to say? | ne zlYsya | ne bIisya | ne plAch | molodEts!
test: In a cartoon, a kid shouts "ya znAyu!" when he doesn't know the answer. What is he claiming? | That he knows the answer | That he doesn't know | That he is bored | That he needs the toilet
test: Sashko got a 12 and won a match on the same day. Which word fits best? | shchaslYvyi | meni sumno | ya zlyi | ya vtomYvsya
test: Your friend snaps "ya zlyi!" and slams his bag down. What is the best next step? | Ask "chomu?" and give him a minute | Laugh and call him a baby | Tell him "molodEts!" | Say "ya znAyu!"
exam: Identify the utterance whereby a Ukrainian pupil customarily signifies, with raised hand, possession of the correct answer. | ya znAyu! | ne znAyu | meni nUdno | shcho stAlosya?
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the construction "meni strAshno" when rendered literally and in its customary sense. | Literally "to me it is frightening", meaning "I am scared" | Literally "I frighten", meaning "I am frightening" | Literally "to me it is boring", meaning "I am bored" | Literally "I am glad", meaning "I am happy"
exam: It is given that a female speaker wishes to declare herself glad. Select the form appropriate to her. | ya rada | ya radyi | ya zlyi | ya vtomYvsya
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the phrase "shcho stAlosya?" | It is a solicitous enquiry as to what has occurred | It is a reprimand for misconduct | It is an enquiry as to the hour | It is a valediction upon departure
